The ACME directory the certificate of the sec is issued by, renewed by
`om <path> certificate renew` or a `task.acme` resource. `letsencrypt` and
`letsencrypt-staging` name the directories of Let's Encrypt, and any other
value is the url of a directory.

The domains are the `cn` and the `alt_names` of the sec, and the account is
registered there, with its `email` as the contact when it has one.

A sec naming no directory has its certificate generated as `certificate
create` does, self-signed or signed by its `ca`, and renewed the same way when
due.

Use the staging directory of Let's Encrypt to try a setup out: its
certificates are not trusted, and its rate limits are far higher.

A certificate of Let's Encrypt for two names, its http-01 challenges served
by the http server of the domains from a webroot:

```
[DEFAULT]
cn = www.example.com
alt_names = example.com
email = admin@example.com

[acme]
directory = letsencrypt
webroot = /srv/web.ns1.vol.cluster1/acme-challenges
```

The same, tried out first on the staging directory:

```
[acme]
directory = letsencrypt-staging
webroot = /srv/web.ns1.vol.cluster1/acme-challenges
```

Changing the directory, as from `letsencrypt-staging` to `letsencrypt` once
the setup works, makes the next renewal issue a new certificate from the
directory now named, due or not.

A sec listed in the `listener.tls_secs` of the cluster needs no webroot: the
listener of every node answers its challenges on `listener.acme_port`.

A certificate of the directory of an ACME server of your own, as a smallstep
`step-ca` with an ACME provisioner named `acme`, served with a certificate the
node trusts:

```
[acme]
directory = https://ca.example.com/acme/acme/directory
```

A certificate of a pebble test server, its certificate trusted by the node
too:

```
[acme]
directory = https://pebble.example.com:14000/dir
```

A directory requiring an external account binding, as ZeroSSL and Google
Trust Services do, is not supported: the account is registered without one.
